{"name":"Sherbet Fruit","hex":"#f8c8bb","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sherbet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dessert of frozen fruit juice with a dairy product such as milk added; a sorbet with dairy ingredients.","An effervescent powder made of bicarbonate of soda, sugar and flavourings, intended to be eaten alone or mixed with water to make a drink."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sherbet"},{"term":"fruit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A product of fertilization in a plant, specifically:\n\nThe seed-bearing part of a plant; often edible, colourful, fragrant, and sweet or sour; produced from a floral ovary after fertilization.\nThe spores of cryptogams and their accessory organs.","The seed-bearing part of a plant; often edible, colourful, fragrant, and sweet or sour; produced from a floral ovary after fertilization."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce fruit, seeds, or spores."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fruit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sherbet (frozen dessert)","blocks":[{"text":"Sherbet, sometimes referred to as sherbert, is a frozen dessert made from water, sugar, a dairy product such as cream or milk, and a flavoring – typically fruit juice or purée, wine, liqueur, or occasionally non-fruit flavors such as vanilla, chocolate, or peppermint. It is similar to, but distinct from, sorbet, which lacks dairy. Sherbet has 1%–2% milkfat, much less than ice cream."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Rainbow_Sherbet_%2838251341974%29.jpg/330px-Rainbow_Sherbet_%2838251341974%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherbet_(frozen_dessert)"},{"title":"Sherbet (powder)","blocks":[{"text":"Sherbet, also called Sherbert or Soda powder, is a fizzy, sweet powdered sugar, usually eaten by dipping a lollipop or liquorice stick, using a small spoon, or licking it from a finger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Jars_of_Kali_or_Sherbet_powder.jpg/330px-Jars_of_Kali_or_Sherbet_powder.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherbet_(powder)"},{"title":"Fruit","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a fruit is the seed-bearing structure in flowering plants (angiosperms) that is formed from the ovary after flowering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Fruit_Platter-_Seasonal_Fruits.jpg/330px-Fruit_Platter-_Seasonal_Fruits.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruit"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Brausepulver.jpg/500px-Brausepulver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Brausepulver.jpg/500px-Brausepulver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"German Brausepulver is similar, and while originally sold  as such is often not mixed with water nowadays but eaten by children by dipping a wet finger into it, or by adults in combination with vodka.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrausepulver.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg/500px-Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg/1280px-Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg/500px-Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sherbet lemons","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASherbet_lemons_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"